From 53ea1e8d499659a734b65a95b8262ee492f8b3ff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 15:29:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 21/31] windows: add a Winsock AF_UNIX backend for the
 virtio-net unixstream link

This is the last device between libkrun and a VM that boots on Windows with a
policy-enforced network. The wire protocol is untouched and must stay untouched:
each Ethernet frame preceded by its length as a 4-byte big-endian integer and
nothing else, which is byte for byte gvisor-tap-vsock's `qemu` protocol. No
vfkit magic, no virtio-net header on the socket.

AF_UNIX rather than a named pipe, for the reason vsock/winsock_unix.rs gives at
length: the peer creates its end in platform-neutral Go with net.Listen("unix",
path), which Go implements with Winsock AF_UNIX on Windows, so a \\.\pipe\
endpoint on our side would never meet it. Readiness reaches the epoll shim the
same way too -- WSAEventSelect() binds the socket to a Windows Event, and that
Event, never the socket, is what gets registered.

Two things differ from that template, both deliberate:

The connect is done while the socket is still blocking, before the event
association exists, so a proxy that is not listening is reported synchronously
from open() exactly as it is on Unix. A guest-IPC vsock port has to report that
asynchronously to the guest; this link has no such caller.

The receive path is a resumable state machine rather than a translation of
unixstream.rs's read_loop(). That loop completes a frame with recv(MSG_WAITALL)
on a blocking socket, and neither half survives here: WSAEventSelect() forces
the socket non-blocking, and MSG_WAITALL is documented to fail with
WSAEOPNOTSUPP on a non-blocking socket. Emulating the wait with WSAPoll() would
block the single worker thread inside read_frame(), stopping the transmit path
with it, and any bounded timeout on that poll leaves us holding half a frame --
which needs exactly the partial-frame state kept in the struct. So read_frame()
never waits: it takes what Winsock has, keeps the partial length prefix and body
across calls, and reports NothingRead until a whole frame is in hand, which the
worker already treats as "stop, you will be woken again".

Framing is the security boundary here rather than a detail. The peer is the host
stack that decides what the guest may reach, so a desynchronised stream would
deliver bytes every policy check upstream still approves, only cut in the wrong
places. The declared length is therefore validated before it is acted on, and
the backend latches itself broken instead of resynchronising by guesswork.

The worker needed two Windows-only changes to drive this. The shim's wait()
reports back the interest mask that was registered rather than observed
readiness -- a wait completion packet only says the Event was signaled -- so the
real state is asked of Winsock through the new take_backend_events(). And
because that call is what resets the Event, the watch must stay edge-triggered
and be re-armed after every wake-up; without the re-arm the link goes deaf after
one frame, and level-triggered would spin at 100% CPU instead.

Compiled for x86_64-pc-windows-msvc and never run. There is no Windows host
here, no VM has been booted against it, and not one frame has moved through it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
