+++ b/src/devices/src/virtio/net/winsock_unixstream.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,583 @@
+//! The Windows end of the virtio-net `unixstream` link.
+//!
+//! This is `unixstream.rs` translated to Winsock. The wire protocol is
+//! unchanged and must stay unchanged: every Ethernet frame is preceded by its
+//! length as a **4-byte big-endian** integer and nothing else, which is byte for
+//! byte gvisor-tap-vsock's `qemu` protocol. Neither the vfkit magic sequence nor
+//! a virtio-net header is ever put on this socket.
+//!
+//! # Why AF_UNIX
+//!
+//! For the same reason `vsock/winsock_unix.rs` gives, and with the same peer
+//! shape: the process on the other end creates its socket in platform-neutral
+//! Go with `net.Listen("unix", path)` on an ordinary filesystem path, which Go
+//! implements with Winsock AF_UNIX on Windows. A `\\.\pipe\...` endpoint on our
+//! side would never meet it. Windows has had AF_UNIX since Windows 10 1803, and
+//! only for `SOCK_STREAM` -- which is exactly the socket type this backend
+//! wants, and the reason its `unixgram` sibling has no Windows counterpart at
+//! all.
+//!
+//! # How readiness reaches the epoll shim
+//!
+//! Identical to `vsock/winsock_unix.rs`, and the reasoning there applies here
+//! unchanged: a Winsock `SOCKET` is not a dispatcher object whose signaled state
+//! means "readable", so it cannot be handed to `utils::windows::epoll`, which
+//! bridges *waitable handles* into an IOCP. `WSAEventSelect()` supplies a
+//! Windows Event that is signaled when the selected network events occur, and
+//! that Event -- never the socket -- is what [`NetBackend::raw_socket_fd`]
+//! returns and what the worker registers.
+//!
+//! Two consequences, both load-bearing:
+//!
+//! 1. The shim's `wait()` reports back the interest mask that was registered,
+//!    not any observed readiness. The real state is recovered with
+//!    `WSAEnumNetworkEvents()`, which is what [`take_backend_events`] does.
+//! 2. That call is also what resets the Event, so the watch must be registered
+//!    `EDGE_TRIGGERED` and re-armed after every enumerate. Level-triggered would
+//!    let the shim re-associate the wait packet before we enumerate, and spin.
+//!
+//! `WSAEventSelect()` additionally forces the socket into non-blocking mode.
+//! That is convenient for the send path -- it is what `MSG_DONTWAIT` buys
+//! `unixstream.rs` -- and it is the reason the receive path below looks
+//! different from the Unix one.
+//!
+//! # Why the receive path is a resumable state machine
+//!
+//! `unixstream.rs` reads a frame in two steps: one non-blocking `recv()` to see
+//! whether anything has arrived, then `recv(MSG_WAITALL)` on a **blocking**
+//! socket to sit there until the rest of the frame has been collected. Neither
+//! half of that is available here.
+//!
+//! * There is no `MSG_DONTWAIT` on Winsock -- immaterial, since
+//!   `WSAEventSelect()` has already made the socket non-blocking.
+//! * `MSG_WAITALL` exists but is documented to fail with `WSAEOPNOTSUPP` on a
+//!   non-blocking socket, and the socket cannot be made blocking again without
+//!   dissolving the event association the poller depends on.
+//!
+//! The remaining option would be to emulate the wait with `WSAPoll()` in a loop.
+//! That is rejected on two counts. It would block the single virtio-net worker
+//! thread inside `read_frame()`, which also stops the transmit path, for as long
+//! as the peer takes to finish a frame -- and any bounded timeout on that poll
+//! leaves us holding half a frame, which needs precisely the partial-frame state
+//! kept below. So the state is cheaper than the alternative *and* strictly
+//! stronger.
+//!
+//! So: `read_frame()` never waits. It consumes whatever Winsock has, keeps the
+//! partially received length prefix and frame body in this struct, and reports
+//! [`ReadError::NothingRead`] until a whole frame is in hand. The worker's
+//! `process_rx()` already treats `NothingRead` as "stop, you will be woken
+//! again", so an incomplete frame simply resumes on the next `FD_READ`.
+//!
+//! The wake-up cannot be lost in between: Winsock re-enables `FD_READ` recording
+//! on *any* call to the re-enabling routine (`recv`) "even one that fails", so
+//! the `recv()` that returned `WSAEWOULDBLOCK` is itself the re-arm on the
+//! Winsock side, and the worker re-arms the shim's wait packet after we return.
+//! The same rule covers `FD_WRITE` and the `send()` that returns
+//! `WSAEWOULDBLOCK` during a partial write.
+//!
+//! # Framing is a security boundary here
+//!
+//! The peer is the host network stack that decides what the guest is allowed to
+//! reach. A desynchronised stream would still deliver bytes that every policy
+//! check upstream approves, only cut in the wrong places, so the length prefix
+//! is validated before it is acted on and the backend latches itself broken
+//! rather than resynchronising by guesswork.
+//!
+//! # Untested
+//!
+//! Nothing in this file has been executed. It has been compiled for
+//! `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc` and never run: there is no Windows host in the
+//! development environment, and no VM has been booted against it, so not one
+//! frame has moved through this code.
+
+use std::io;
+use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};
+use std::sync::Once;
+
+use windows_sys::Win32::Networking::WinSock::{
+    AF_UNIX, FD_CLOSE, FD_READ, FD_WRITE, INVALID_SOCKET, SO_RCVBUF, SO_SNDBUF, SOCK_STREAM,
+    SOCKADDR, SOCKADDR_UN, SOCKET, SOCKET_ERROR, SOL_SOCKET, WSACloseEvent, WSACreateEvent,
+    WSADATA, WSAECONNABORTED, WSAECONNRESET, WSAENETRESET, WSAENOTCONN, WSAESHUTDOWN,
+    WSAEWOULDBLOCK, WSAEnumNetworkEvents, WSAEventSelect, WSAGetLastError, WSANETWORKEVENTS,
+    WSAStartup, closesocket, connect, getsockopt, recv, send, setsockopt, socket,
+};
+
+use utils::epoll::EventSet;
+use utils::windows::RawFd;
+
+use super::backend::{ConnectError, NetBackend, ReadError, WriteError};
+use super::write_virtio_net_hdr;
+use super::{MAX_BUFFER_SIZE, VNET_HDR_LEN};
+
+/// Each frame the network proxy is prepended by a 4 byte "header".
+/// It is interpreted as a big-endian u32 integer and is the length of the following ethernet frame.
+const FRAME_HEADER_LEN: usize = 4;
+
+/// The largest frame body that can still be handed to the guest once the
+/// virtio-net header has been written in front of it. A declared length above
+/// this cannot be a frame we could deliver, so it is treated as a desynchronised
+/// stream rather than as a very large frame.
+const MAX_FRAME_LEN: usize = MAX_BUFFER_SIZE - VNET_HDR_LEN;
+
+/// The network events this link cares about. `FD_WRITE` is selected so that a
+/// send blocked by a full socket buffer is resumed when space appears; it is the
+/// Winsock equivalent of the `EPOLLOUT` the Unix worker relies on to finish a
+/// partial write.
+const LINK_EVENTS: i32 = (FD_READ | FD_WRITE | FD_CLOSE) as i32;
+
+/// Winsock must be initialised once per process before any other ws2_32 call.
+/// `std::net` does this lazily for its own sockets, but nothing guarantees a
+/// `std` socket has been created by the time a network device is configured.
+fn winsock_init() {
+    static START: Once = Once::new();
+    START.call_once(|| {
+        let mut data: WSADATA = unsafe { std::mem::zeroed() };
+        // 2.2 is the highest version Winsock defines and the one every
+        // supported Windows release provides.
+        let ret = unsafe { WSAStartup(0x0202, &mut data) };
+        if ret != 0 {
+            log::error!("WSAStartup failed: {ret}");
+        }
+    });
+}
+
+fn last_wsa_error() -> i32 {
+    unsafe { WSAGetLastError() }
+}
+
+fn wsa_io_error() -> io::Error {
+    io::Error::from_raw_os_error(last_wsa_error())
+}
+
+/// Build a `SOCKADDR_UN` for `path`.
+///
+/// Windows' `sun_path` is 108 bytes including the NUL and is interpreted as a
+/// byte string in the system ANSI code page rather than as UTF-16. Paths that
+/// are not representable there cannot be addressed; that is a Winsock AF_UNIX
+/// limitation, not one this function introduces. Go's `net` package writes the
+/// same bytes, so the two ends agree for any path either can express.
+fn sockaddr_un(path: &Path) -> Result<(SOCKADDR_UN, i32), ConnectError> {
+    let bytes = path.as_os_str().to_str().map(str::as_bytes).ok_or_else(|| {
+        ConnectError::InvalidAddress(io::Error::from(io::ErrorKind::InvalidInput))
+    })?;
+
+    let mut addr = SOCKADDR_UN {
+        sun_family: AF_UNIX,
+        sun_path: [0; 108],
+    };
+    if bytes.len() >= addr.sun_path.len() {
+        return Err(ConnectError::InvalidAddress(io::Error::from(
+            io::ErrorKind::InvalidFilename,
+        )));
+    }
+    for (slot, byte) in addr.sun_path.iter_mut().zip(bytes) {
+        *slot = *byte as i8;
+    }
+
+    // The length passed to connect() covers the family, the path and its
+    // terminating NUL -- not the whole fixed-size array.
+    let len = (std::mem::size_of::<u16>() + bytes.len() + 1) as i32;
+    Ok((addr, len))
+}
+
+/// `setsockopt(SOL_SOCKET, opt, value)`, logged rather than fatal.
+///
+/// `unixstream.rs` treats a failure to grow the send buffer as a performance
+/// note, not an error, and so does this.
+fn set_buf_size(sock: SOCKET, opt: i32, name: &str, value: i32) {
+    let ret = unsafe {
+        setsockopt(
+            sock,
+            SOL_SOCKET,
+            opt,
+            &value as *const i32 as *const u8,
+            std::mem::size_of::<i32>() as i32,
+        )
+    };
+    if ret == SOCKET_ERROR {
+        log::warn!(
+            "Failed to set {name} (performance may be decreased): {}",
+            last_wsa_error()
+        );
+    }
+}
+
+/// One non-blocking `recv()` into `dst`.
+///
+/// `Ok(Some(n))` always has `n > 0`. `Ok(None)` is `WSAEWOULDBLOCK`: nothing
+/// more is buffered, every byte of partial-frame state stays intact for the next
+/// wake-up, and -- per Winsock's rule that *any* call to the re-enabling routine
+/// re-arms the notification, "even one that fails" -- this very call is what
+/// guarantees `FD_READ` fires again. `Err` covers both a socket error and a
+/// peer that closed (`recv` returning 0): no more bytes will ever arrive, so a
+/// half-received frame can never be completed.
+///
+/// A free function rather than a method so that callers can pass a mutable
+/// borrow of one of their own fields as `dst`.
+fn recv_some(sock: SOCKET, dst: &mut [u8]) -> Result<Option<usize>, io::Error> {
+    let cnt = unsafe { recv(sock, dst.as_mut_ptr(), dst.len() as i32, 0) };
+    match cnt {
+        SOCKET_ERROR => {
+            let err = last_wsa_error();
+            if err == WSAEWOULDBLOCK {
+                Ok(None)
+            } else {
+                Err(io::Error::from_raw_os_error(err))
+            }
+        }
+        0 => Err(io::Error::from(io::ErrorKind::UnexpectedEof)),
+        n => Ok(Some(n as usize)),
+    }
+}
+
+fn get_buf_size(sock: SOCKET, opt: i32) -> io::Result<i32> {
+    let mut value: i32 = 0;
+    let mut len = std::mem::size_of::<i32>() as i32;
+    let ret = unsafe {
+        getsockopt(
+            sock,
+            SOL_SOCKET,
+            opt,
+            &mut value as *mut i32 as *mut u8,
+            &mut len,
+        )
+    };
+    if ret == SOCKET_ERROR {
+        Err(wsa_io_error())
+    } else {
+        Ok(value)
+    }
+}
+
+pub struct WinsockUnixstream {
+    sock: SOCKET,
+    /// The Event `WSAEventSelect()` signals for `sock`. This, not `sock`, is
+    /// what the worker registers with the epoll shim.
+    event: isize,
+
+    /// The length prefix of the frame currently being received, and how much of
+    /// it has arrived. A prefix is complete exactly when `hdr_filled` reaches
+    /// `FRAME_HEADER_LEN`; there is no sentinel length value, so a peer cannot
+    /// reset our parser state by declaring one.
+    hdr_buf: [u8; FRAME_HEADER_LEN],
+    hdr_filled: usize,
+
+    /// The body of the frame currently being received.
+    ///
+    /// Staged here rather than accumulated straight into the caller's buffer
+    /// because [`NetBackend::read_frame`] promises nothing about that buffer
+    /// surviving unchanged across the calls a partially received frame spans.
+    /// The copy costs one `memcpy` of a frame per frame.
+    frame_buf: Box<[u8]>,
+    frame_filled: usize,
+
+    /// Latched when the stream can no longer be trusted -- a bad length prefix,
+    /// a peer that closed mid-frame, or a socket error. Reads never resume after
+    /// this: resynchronising a length-prefixed stream by guesswork would feed
+    /// the host network stack frames cut in places the guest chose.
+    broken: bool,
+
+    /// 0 if the last write is fully complete, otherwise the length that was written
+    last_partial_write_length: usize,
+}
+
+impl WinsockUnixstream {
+    /// Create the backend opening a connection to the userspace network proxy.
+    ///
+    /// The connect happens while the socket is still blocking and *before* the
+    /// event association is made, so a proxy that is not listening is reported
+    /// here, synchronously, exactly as it is on Unix. (`vsock/winsock_unix.rs`
+    /// arms first and connects later because a guest-IPC port must report the
+    /// outcome to the guest asynchronously; this link has no such caller.)
+    pub fn open(path: PathBuf) -> Result<Self, ConnectError> {
+        winsock_init();
+
+        let sock = unsafe { socket(AF_UNIX as i32, SOCK_STREAM, 0) };
+        if sock == INVALID_SOCKET {
+            return Err(ConnectError::CreateSocket(wsa_io_error()));
+        }
+
+        let (addr, addr_len) = match sockaddr_un(&path) {
+            Ok(a) => a,
+            Err(e) => {
+                unsafe { closesocket(sock) };
+                return Err(e);
+            }
+        };
+
+        if unsafe {
+            connect(
+                sock,
+                &addr as *const SOCKADDR_UN as *const SOCKADDR,
+                addr_len,
+            )
+        } == SOCKET_ERROR
+        {
+            let err = wsa_io_error();
+            unsafe { closesocket(sock) };
+            return Err(ConnectError::Binding(err));
+        }
+
+        set_buf_size(sock, SO_SNDBUF, "SO_SNDBUF", 16 * 1024 * 1024);
+
+        log::debug!(
+            "network proxy socket (sock {sock:?}) buffer sizes: SndBuf={:?} RcvBuf={:?}",
+            get_buf_size(sock, SO_SNDBUF),
+            get_buf_size(sock, SO_RCVBUF)
+        );
+
+        // Arm last: this is also what switches the socket to non-blocking, and
+        // everything above wanted blocking semantics.
+        let event = unsafe { WSACreateEvent() };
+        // WSACreateEvent returns WSA_INVALID_EVENT, which is 0, on failure.
+        if event == 0 {
+            let err = wsa_io_error();
+            unsafe { closesocket(sock) };
+            return Err(ConnectError::CreateSocket(err));
+        }
+        if unsafe { WSAEventSelect(sock, event, LINK_EVENTS) } == SOCKET_ERROR {
+            let err = wsa_io_error();
+            unsafe {
+                WSACloseEvent(event);
+                closesocket(sock);
+            }
+            return Err(ConnectError::CreateSocket(err));
+        }
+
+        Ok(Self {
+            sock,
+            event,
+            hdr_buf: [0u8; FRAME_HEADER_LEN],
+            hdr_filled: 0,
+            frame_buf: vec![0u8; MAX_FRAME_LEN].into_boxed_slice(),
+            frame_filled: 0,
+            broken: false,
+            last_partial_write_length: 0,
+        })
+    }
+
+    /// Mark the link unusable and produce the error to report for it.
+    fn fail(&mut self, err: io::Error) -> ReadError {
+        self.broken = true;
+        ReadError::Internal(err)
+    }
+
+    /// The declared body length of the frame being received, validated.
+    ///
+    /// Only called once the whole prefix is in hand.
+    fn declared_frame_len(&self) -> Result<usize, io::Error> {
+        let len = u32::from_be_bytes(self.hdr_buf) as usize;
+        // A zero-length frame carries no Ethernet header and a frame longer
+        // than the receive buffer cannot be delivered. Either means the stream
+        // is no longer where we think it is; there is nothing to recover.
+        if len == 0 || len > MAX_FRAME_LEN {
+            return Err(io::Error::other(format!(
+                "network proxy declared a {len} byte frame (limit {MAX_FRAME_LEN}); \
+                 the length-prefixed stream is desynchronised"
+            )));
+        }
+        Ok(len)
+    }
+
+    fn write_loop(&mut self, buf: &[u8]) -> Result<(), WriteError> {
+        let mut bytes_sent = 0;
+
+        while bytes_sent < buf.len() {
+            let rest = &buf[bytes_sent..];
+            let cnt = unsafe { send(self.sock, rest.as_ptr(), rest.len() as i32, 0) };
+
+            if cnt == SOCKET_ERROR {
+                let err = last_wsa_error();
+                if err == WSAEWOULDBLOCK {
+                    if bytes_sent == 0 {
+                        return Err(WriteError::NothingWritten);
+                    }
+                    log::trace!(
+                        "Wrote {bytes_sent} bytes, but socket blocked, will need try_finish_write() to finish"
+                    );
+                    self.last_partial_write_length += bytes_sent;
+                    return Err(WriteError::PartialWrite);
+                }
+                // The Winsock spellings of "the peer is gone". `unixstream.rs`
+                // recognises this as EPIPE.
+                return Err(
+                    if matches!(
+                        err,
+                        WSAECONNRESET | WSAECONNABORTED | WSAESHUTDOWN | WSAENETRESET | WSAENOTCONN
+                    ) {
+                        WriteError::ProcessNotRunning
+                    } else {
+                        WriteError::Internal(io::Error::from_raw_os_error(err))
+                    },
+                );
+            }
+
+            if cnt == 0 {
+                // Not documented to happen for a non-empty stream send, but
+                // looping on it would spin forever. Treat it as the socket
+                // refusing more, which the caller already knows how to resume.
+                if bytes_sent == 0 {
+                    return Err(WriteError::NothingWritten);
+                }
+                self.last_partial_write_length += bytes_sent;
+                return Err(WriteError::PartialWrite);
+            }
+
+            bytes_sent += cnt as usize;
+        }
+
+        self.last_partial_write_length = 0;
+        Ok(())
+    }
+}
+
+impl Drop for WinsockUnixstream {
+    fn drop(&mut self) {
+        unsafe {
+            // Cancel the association before tearing either object down, so no
+            // further packets can be queued against a dead watch.
+            WSAEventSelect(self.sock, 0, 0);
+            WSACloseEvent(self.event);
+            closesocket(self.sock);
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+impl NetBackend for WinsockUnixstream {
+    /// Try to read a frame from the proxy.
+    ///
+    /// Reports [`ReadError::NothingRead`] both when nothing has arrived at all
+    /// and when only part of a frame has: in either case the caller should stop
+    /// and wait to be woken, and the frame in progress resumes on the next call.
+    fn read_frame(&mut self, buf: &mut [u8]) -> Result<usize, ReadError> {
+        if self.broken {
+            return Err(ReadError::Internal(io::Error::other(
+                "virtio-net link is broken; no further frames will be read",
+            )));
+        }
+
+        // Complete the 4-byte big-endian length prefix. Anything already
+        // collected by an earlier call stays where it is.
+        while self.hdr_filled < FRAME_HEADER_LEN {
+            let filled = self.hdr_filled;
+            match recv_some(self.sock, &mut self.hdr_buf[filled..]) {
+                Ok(Some(n)) => self.hdr_filled += n,
+                Ok(None) => return Err(ReadError::NothingRead),
+                Err(e) => return Err(self.fail(e)),
+            }
+        }
+
+        let frame_length = match self.declared_frame_len() {
+            Ok(len) => len,
+            Err(e) => return Err(self.fail(e)),
+        };
+
+        // Collect the body, likewise resuming where the last call stopped.
+        while self.frame_filled < frame_length {
+            let filled = self.frame_filled;
+            match recv_some(self.sock, &mut self.frame_buf[filled..frame_length]) {
+                Ok(Some(n)) => self.frame_filled += n,
+                Ok(None) => return Err(ReadError::NothingRead),
+                Err(e) => return Err(self.fail(e)),
+            }
+        }
+
+        // Checked before `write_virtio_net_hdr()`, which indexes `buf`
+        // unconditionally. `declared_frame_len()` has already bounded
+        // `frame_length` by `MAX_FRAME_LEN`, so this only fires if the caller
+        // passed a buffer smaller than `MAX_BUFFER_SIZE`.
+        if buf.len() < VNET_HDR_LEN + frame_length {
+            return Err(self.fail(io::Error::other(
+                "receive buffer is smaller than the frame the proxy sent",
+            )));
+        }
+        let hdr_len = write_virtio_net_hdr(buf);
+        buf[hdr_len..hdr_len + frame_length].copy_from_slice(&self.frame_buf[..frame_length]);
+
+        self.hdr_filled = 0;
+        self.frame_filled = 0;
+
+        log::trace!("Read eth frame from network proxy: {frame_length} bytes");
+        Ok(hdr_len + frame_length)
+    }
+
+    /// Try to write a frame to the proxy.
+    /// (Will mutate and override parts of buf, with a frame header!)
+    ///
+    /// * `hdr_len` - specifies the size of any existing headers encapsulating the ethernet frame,
+    ///   (such as vnet header), that can be overwritten. Must be >= FRAME_HEADER_LEN.
+    /// * `buf` - the buffer to write to the proxy, `buf[..hdr_len]` may be overwritten
+    ///
+    /// If this function returns WriteError::PartialWrite, you have to finish the write using
+    /// try_finish_write.
+    fn write_frame(&mut self, hdr_len: usize, buf: &mut [u8]) -> Result<(), WriteError> {
+        if self.last_partial_write_length != 0 {
+            panic!("Cannot write a frame to the proxy, while a partial write is not resolved.");
+        }
+        assert!(
+            hdr_len >= FRAME_HEADER_LEN,
+            "Not enough space to write the frame header"
+        );
+        assert!(buf.len() > hdr_len);
+        let frame_length = buf.len() - hdr_len;
+
+        buf[hdr_len - FRAME_HEADER_LEN..hdr_len]
+            .copy_from_slice(&(frame_length as u32).to_be_bytes());
+
+        self.write_loop(&buf[hdr_len - FRAME_HEADER_LEN..])?;
+        Ok(())
+    }
+
+    fn has_unfinished_write(&self) -> bool {
+        self.last_partial_write_length != 0
+    }
+
+    /// Try to finish a partial write
+    ///
+    /// If no partial write is required will do nothing and return Ok(())
+    ///
+    /// * `hdr_len` - must be the same value as passed to write_frame, that caused the partial write
+    /// * `buf` - must be same buffer that was given to write_frame, that caused the partial write
+    fn try_finish_write(&mut self, hdr_len: usize, buf: &[u8]) -> Result<(), WriteError> {
+        if self.last_partial_write_length != 0 {
+            let already_written = self.last_partial_write_length;
+            log::trace!("Requested to finish partial write");
+            self.write_loop(&buf[hdr_len - FRAME_HEADER_LEN + already_written..])?;
+            log::debug!("Finished partial write ({already_written}bytes written before)")
+        }
+
+        Ok(())
+    }
+
+    /// The Event, not the socket -- see the module docs.
+    fn raw_socket_fd(&self) -> RawFd {
+        self.event as RawFd
+    }
+
+    fn take_backend_events(&mut self) -> EventSet {
+        let mut events: WSANETWORKEVENTS = unsafe { std::mem::zeroed() };
+        if unsafe { WSAEnumNetworkEvents(self.sock, self.event, &mut events) } == SOCKET_ERROR {
+            log::warn!("WSAEnumNetworkEvents failed: {}", last_wsa_error());
+            return EventSet::empty();
+        }
+
+        let fired = events.lNetworkEvents as u32;
+        let mut set = EventSet::empty();
+
+        if fired & FD_READ != 0 {
+            set |= EventSet::IN;
+        }
+        if fired & FD_WRITE != 0 {
+            set |= EventSet::OUT;
+        }
+        if fired & FD_CLOSE != 0 {
+            // `iErrorCode[FD_CLOSE_BIT]` would tell an orderly shutdown from an
+            // abortive one, but the worker does the same thing either way, and
+            // the same thing Linux does for EPOLLHUP/EPOLLRDHUP: the network
+            // proxy is gone and networking stops.
+            set |= EventSet::HANG_UP;
+        }
+
+        set
+    }
+}
